{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact post-landing corrections draft 2 ab41ab96643ea90be6034273c85ea06112005534/tree 10a0a56d755e77f0e0e9c9173c0858b02e327c8b. The request metadata is now correct: checker owes no landing, and the native primary and requested reading primary both name sequenced artifact 6046fa01. I read that 1054-line note FIRST in full, then the full 700-line packages note and 156-line Plan 033 amendment, and checked all three exact artifacts. The findings recorded as data in ineffective report 44ad8b0d were useful inputs; this is a new guarded verdict, not a claim that the old report became effective.\n\nF1 (P1, remaining part of prior F2), notes/2026-10-18-sequenced-level-and-order-scope.md:415 and :695. Define and verify the outside-order refusal exception. Step 3 now requires a sequenced handler receiving order-left or not-a-member to write an ordinary deciding entry and authenticated result outside the order; the new cost row at 717 likewise charges one member entry. But 5.8 still says that an entry of a sequenced scope whose input is sequenced and has no position is a mismatch. A concrete allowed trace is leave(M) at L, then a verified request to M's sequenced handler: step 3 writes a refused delivery with no allocation/position, and the stated replay rule rejects exactly that journal. Calling it \"as an unsequenced delivery\" does not define a distinguishable retained entry or a replay exception for the same originally sequenced handler. This is now narrower than the original post-allocation problem, which the prepare/commit change resolves.\n\nSpecify the entry/provenance and replay rule for this case: what retained order/leave or checked not-a-member fact proves the exception, how the verifier derives this terminal refusal without allocation or group-page reads, and how the deciding-entry index/result remains the effective fact. Do not exempt every missing-position input based solely on its claimed reason. S3/S4/S5 need a matching positive native/replay witness for the allowed outside-order refusal, a repeat/result witness, and a negative case that removes or forges the justification while an ordinary sequenced applied entry without a position still fails. Alternatively choose another explicit pre-allocation policy and propagate it through the turn, costs and witnesses. As written, S4's new leave-refusal witness and S5's verifier cannot both pass their stated rules.\n\nF2 (P2), sequenced note:189 and :923, and packages note:526-528. Update the current protocol summaries to the adopted act/delivery distinction. Section 5.1 still says a refused input leaves a hole; decision 7 still says every refused sequenced input is a hole and a hole never gains a commit; packages section 9 still says refusals are recorded only in the order scope, never in a member's journal. These are presented as current rules and dependencies, but new 5.2/5.4 require a refused delivery to prepare, resolve and commit in the member. Scope the hole-only statements to refused acts after allocation, describe delivery refusal through its deciding commit, and include the agreed outside-order case once F1 is resolved. Keep older wording only when clearly marked as historical. Otherwise implementers can follow two incompatible protocols in this same commissioned design.\n\nThe substantive corrections otherwise address the earlier findings. Prior F1: a post-allocation refused delivery now shares the durable prepare, fence, reservation and resume path, so an unsequenced head cannot hide a lost hole-release duty. Prior F3: write-time fits is rerun; before a prepare, capacity failure answers a delivery unavailable and preserves its sender's duty rather than manufacturing an unfunded terminal result. Prior F4: late and spacing are request-handler forms only, with explicit advisory rejection. I checked the actual native advisory path and section-7.4 request decision/result path in derive/delivery.ts at Main 1da3f4ca. This is a design review; no sequenced runtime is implemented or executed here.\n\nArchitecture: changes required for F1 and F2. The unified prepare/commit path is a substantial simplification and preserves the frozen judgment time/prospective sequence model for refused deliveries. The one outside-order terminal path must have an equally explicit replay contract. Platform scopes remain excluded by 5.10/O3; no platform sequencing or reserved native settlement composition is approved by this review.\n\nSecurity: the declaration-level advisory restriction is sound and avoids inventing authenticated advisory results. Existing full-member identity, group authorization, finality, retained-page domains and effective-fact checks remain required. F1 needs authenticated retained justification for its exception; a reason label alone is insufficient.\n\nSimplification: reuse the existing prepare/fence/resolve/commit for all post-allocation delivery judgments, as this revision proposes, and state the small pre-allocation exception once. Align the three summaries rather than adding a parallel release/recovery mechanism.\n\nPlan 033 decision 7 and slice 9 are byte-identical to the prior reviewed correction: binding in the directory, hosting policy/configuration, real destination/token, A3 wiring and disclosure remain separate prerequisites; G5 -\u003e demo-room archive -\u003e G4 and the A2/A3 fixture-only cutoff are preserved.\n\nValidation: /private/tmp/checker-post-landing-docs-draft2-v84atd78/validation.json verifies all 2249 leaves/modes/blob IDs against the candidate, 2246 prior leaves unchanged against 165d5a86/tree 1b69aa4787f8a64fca1bc299567c886527bdc27f, and exactly three documentation paths changed. Source and tests are unchanged; whitespace passes, so no gate/suite was repeated under docs/testing.md's documentation-only rule. design-traces.json records the explicit leave/refusal/replay counterexample and the summary conflicts as manual protocol traces, not runtime evidence. Planner amendment 9ae4eb97 and survey 40cfeb9a were read in full; they adopt the same outside-order case but do not supply its missing retained/replay exception. No source edit, ref mutation, package install, push or deployment was performed by checker.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6046fa010f5dc7dc14565076b2d459be80d6c254","binding":"assigned","head":"ab41ab96643ea90be6034273c85ea06112005534","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dcf2952fdea23777f7f3f32215f6fb3f627e382e\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ebdd29c9480ba58ce74fdf0fd6e531887fb67b1a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c7639e2e9fd484336912c3c3bb5830942bf10259","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}